      'Irked is a retired HackTheBox easy Linux machine whose name tells the story: an IRC daemon with feelings (and a backdoor). nmap shows UnrealIRCd 3.2.8.1 on the IRC port — a version famous for a planted backdoor (CVE-2010-2075) that executes any AB;-prefixed message as a shell command. Service version plus one search equals foothold.',
      'Foothold: netcat to the IRC port, complete a minimal handshake, then send AB; followed by a reverse-shell one-liner. The daemon executes it and calls back — a shell as the low-privilege ircd user. Backdoored software is the extreme end of supply-chain risk, and this box makes the mechanics unforgettable.',
      'Lateral data: the home directories hold a user plus a steganography-flavored backup file — extract it (steghide with the discoverable passphrase) for the djmarduk credential and step up to a fuller user shell with more enumeration rights.',
      'Privilege escalation via SUID viewuser: a custom SUID-root binary displays a user list file from /tmp — and follows symlinks without checking. Replace /tmp/listusers with a link to /root/root.txt and the binary prints the flag for you; the same primitive plus a shell upgrade path yields full root. SUID binaries that read files are file-read vulnerabilities wearing a uniform.',
      'Flags: user.txt in the user home directory, root.txt via the viewuser trick. (Values redacted per HTB rules; earn them in your own lab.)',
      'Dead ends I hit so you do not have to: interacting with IRC channels socially (skip the chat — the backdoor triggers on raw protocol input, so script the handshake) and ignoring the stego file as decoration (backups and images on easy boxes are credential storage until proven otherwise).',
      'Remediation notes: never run known-backdoored software (inventory IRC and legacy daemons), strip SUID from custom binaries that touch files, and treat any SUID program you did not write as suspicious by default.',
